  {
    "type": "post",
    "id": "7505235033486467072",
    "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/li-user-25_a-test-fails-in-the-pipeline-before-anyone-activity-7505235033486467072-MY1L",
    "content": "A test fails in the pipeline. Before anyone touches the product code, one question needs an answer first: is this a real bug, or an unstable test?\n\nSkipping that question is expensive either way. Treat a flaky test like a bug, and you waste time chasing something that isn't there. Treat a real bug like flakiness, and you ship a broken feature.\n\nThe habit that helps most is collecting evidence before assigning blame: rerun the test in isolation, check if it fails consistently or only under load, compare logs across runs, and look at whether the failure correlates with a specific environment or dataset.\n\nThe classification isn't guesswork. It's a checklist: reproducibility, isolation, environment correlation, and timing.\n\nSkip this step and pipelines get noisier over time, and some real defects slip through with them.",
