  {
    "type": "post",
    "id": "7488483374173569024",
    "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/li-user-58_flakytests-testautomation-softwaretesting-activity-7488483374173569024-WTye",
    "content": "One flaky test fails. You rerun it. It passes. Problem solved? Probably not. 🎭\n\nFlaky tests are often treated as isolated scripts with isolated defects. In practice, several tests may be reacting to the same unstable environment, shared dependency, timing issue, test data problem, or setup process.\n\nFixing them one by one can hide the real pattern.\n\nInstead of looking only at the latest failed test, examine which tests fail together, when the failures started, and what resources they share. Test run history can reveal a cluster that is almost invisible when every failure is investigated separately.\n\nRetries may unblock the pipeline, but they do not restore trust in the test suite. The real improvement comes from finding the shared cause behind the noise.\n\nHow does your team handle flaky tests: rerun, quarantine, or investigate immediately? 👇\n\n#FlakyTests #TestAutomation #SoftwareTesting #QualityEngineering #ContinuousIntegration",
