  {
    "type": "post",
    "id": "7495827503773564928",
    "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/li-user-62_most-teams-migrate-to-playwright-to-escape-activity-7495827503773564928-GTSq",
    "content": "Most teams migrate to Playwright to escape flaky tests.\nThen the flakiness follows them home.\nAnd they blame the tool. 👇\n—\nHere's what I watched happen on one team:\nThey moved thousands of tests off a legacy framework.\nNew syntax. Same 3 a.m. failures. Re-run until green. And slowly… everyone stopped trusting the suite.\nThe tool was never the problem.\nThey'd carried the old habits into a brand-new house:\n→ hard-coded sleeps as \"flakiness insurance\" \n→ selectors glued to the DOM structure \n→ an inverted pyramid — hundreds of slow UI tests checking logic a unit test could've caught in milliseconds \n→ CI runners so starved for memory the browsers were thrashing (which everyone confidently diagnosed as \"Playwright just being flaky\")\nPlaywright didn't fail them.\nTheir engineering discipline did.\n—\nSo I wrote the deep field guide I wish that team had.\nNot a getting-started tutorial — the architectural reasoning for engineers with 5–15 years in the trenches:\n• Why lazy Locators killed the stale-element era (and how ElementHandles still quietly bite) \n• Actionability checks + web-first assertions — the real fix for sleeps \n• Fixtures as dependency injection: worker vs test scope, and why it changes everything \n• The hybrid pattern → arrange state via API, assert via UI \n• Trace Viewer as your 3 a.m. time machine \n• Flakiness as a trust problem — and the metrics that prove it to leadership\n—\nThe uncomfortable truth:\nA technically perfect suite that the org has learned to ignore is worthless.\nThe tool is the easy half.\nThe discipline around it is the actual job.\n—\nFull guide linked below. 👇\nWhat's the #1 flakiness anti-pattern you see most often?\nI'll start: hard-coded sleeps that make suites slow AND flaky at the same time.\n♻️ Repost if your team is fighting a flaky suite right now.",
